18|1. `requestFigure` ignores the `tryStart` return (`app/folio/use-pictures.ts:164`).
19|   What this is: `tryStart(busyRef);` runs, then `poll(...)` starts no
20|   matter what it returned. If it ever returned false, the second request
21|   would still fly, and its `.finally(finish)` would clear `busy` while the
22|   first request is still in flight, opening the gate for a third.
23|   Today it cannot happen: both callers (`illustratePassage` via `canStart`,
24|   `retryFigure` via `canRetryFigure`) check `busy` synchronously in the
25|   same tick, so `tryStart` inside `requestFigure` always succeeds, exactly
26|   like the old unconditional `busyRef.current = true`. Behavior is
27|   identical, but the atomic check-then-act the extraction was meant to buy
28|   lives in the callers instead of the return value.
29|   If we skip it: a future caller that trusts `tryStart` to guard will get
30|   double requests and a corrupted gate. Should fix: check the return
31|   (`if (!tryStart(busyRef)) return;`) and keep a comment that the callers
32|   pre-check, or drop `tryStart` from `requestFigure` and say the callers own
33|   the gate. Note the wrinkle: `illustratePassage` inserts the loading figure
34|   before calling `requestFigure`, so an early return there must run before
35|   the insert, or it orphans a loading figure.
36|2. The `figuresAfter` doc comment contradicts the code and the test
37|   (`app/folio/figure-placement.ts:11-14`).
38|   What this is: the comment says a paragraph split across pages shows its
39|   figures "only on the page holding its last visible fragment there". The
40|   code is per-page-local (`paragraphs[i + 1]?.n !== cur.n`), so each page's
41|   last visible fragment of that ordinal renders the figures, on both pages.
42|   The test (`figure-placement.test.ts:44-53`) pins the true both-pages
43|   behavior, which matches the old inline code exactly.
44|   If we skip it: someone will "fix" the code to match the comment and break
45|   the second page's figures. Should fix: one-line comment correction to say
46|   each page renders after its own last visible fragment.
47|3. `prefRef.current = pref` runs during render (`app/folio/use-pictures.ts:113-114`).
48|   What this is: the ref is written in the render body instead of an effect.
49|   The value read at event time equals the old per-call argument (the caller
50|   passed the current `pref` state at click time; the ref holds the latest
51|   rendered one), so bodies, credit, and timing are identical, and the
52|   callbacks stay stable as claimed. Only concurrent-render tearing could
53|   ever show a stale pref, and this tree has no path that triggers it.
54|   Nice to have: assign in `useEffect`, or keep with a one-line why.
